# Nightly sponsor resolution runtime state.
#
# config/sponsor_norm_v2/ is otherwise TRACKED on purpose — the registry,
# assignments and mint caches are paid LLM output and the seed state a fresh
# checkout needs. But N_nightly_resolve.R writes its own working files into the
# same directory, and those are per-environment runtime state: a work list, a
# retry ledger, a run history, a crash snapshot. On the server they live in
# $SPONSOR_V2_DIR outside the repo; locally they are test noise, and without
# this a `git add -A` would commit one machine's run history as if it were data.
config/sponsor_norm_v2/N_*.csv
config/sponsor_norm_v2/.snapshot/
data/.sponsor_nightly_failed

# Substance emit by-products, regenerated by E_emit.R on every run.
#
# Neither matches data/*log* or data/*labels*, so without this they sit in
# `git status` as untracked forever and a `git add -A` commits one machine's
# run output to main. They reach Posit the same way the label CSVs do: the
# nightly force-adds substance_rejected.csv onto the deploy branch, which is
# also why it must NOT be tracked on main — two sources for the same file is
# how the deploy branch and main start disagreeing about what the pipeline
# decided. The residue file is diagnostics and has no reader at all.
data/substance_rejected.csv
data/substance_residue.csv
